/* Layout for the new-character PROFESSION tab (Tier 4 #4 slice 7).

   The grouped card tree, its floaty info panel and the sigil legend are NOT here: they are
   the shared chooser vocabulary in newchar_common.rcss, which this tab and the SCENARIO tab
   both use so the two screens cannot drift apart. What stays here is this tab's own frame and
   its status rows. */

body {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
}

/* 98%: creation fills the screen, the remaining 1% each side keeping the panel's own border
   visible as a frame. The old 72% existed to dodge a character-preview box drawn as a GPU
   sprite UNDERNEATH this document; the avatar now renders into its own target and arrives as
   a decorator inside the info panel, so there is nothing left outside the panel to occlude.
   Keep all eight tabs identical so the panel edge does not jump while tabbing. */
.nc-panel {
    width: 98%;
    height: 98%;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
}

.nc-steps {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
}

.nc-step {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
}

/* Status row: points, the selected profession's cost, and the sort hint. */
.nc-prof-top {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 6dp 8dp;
}

.nc-prof-points {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-right: 16dp;
}

.nc-prof-cost {
    display: block;
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
}

/* Guaranteed gap: the cost line beside it is `flex: 1`, so a long profession name grows
   until it butts straight into this hint and the two read as one welded string. */
.nc-prof-sort {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-left: 20dp;
    color: {{gray}};
}

/* Holds the shared .nc-tree. A row rather than a column because the tree is the only child:
   this exists so the tree gets the stage's leftover height to centre itself in. */
.nc-prof-body {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
}

.nc-prof-gender {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    padding: 2dp 8dp;
    color: {{gray}};
}

.nc-prof-filter {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    padding: 0 8dp 2dp 8dp;
    color: {{gray}};
}

/* ── STARTING-EQUIPMENT SHEET ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

   The kit the selected profession hands over, in the Caves of Qud idiom: paper doll of body
   slots, collapsible category tree with weight rollups, carry-capacity bar. Kept in THIS
   sheet rather than newchar_common.rcss because PROFESSION is its only adopter; promote it on
   the second one, as the tree vocabulary was — two copies of a visual language diverge.

   It replaces .nc-tree inside .nc-prof-body, so it inherits that row's leftover height. */
/* Opaque ground, like .nc-info-panel has. Without it the curses layer that still draws
   underneath this document (main-menu remnants, the old w_items buffer) showed through the
   sheet's empty right-hand region as ghost text. */
.nc-eqp {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    padding: 4dp 8dp 0 8dp;
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
}

.nc-eqp-head {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding-bottom: 5dp;
    border-bottom: 2dp {{yellow}};
}

.nc-eqp-title {
    display: block;
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    font-size: 16dp;
    font-weight: bold;
    letter-spacing: 1dp;
}

/* One per total. margin-left rather than spaces in the string: RmlUi trims a lone space between
   two colour runs at parse time, which welded "16 items 9.1 kg 9.32 L $2795.26" into one word. */
.nc-eqp-total {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-left: 18dp;
    font-size: 13dp;
}

/* Doll beside tree. The doll is fixed-width so the tree's columns do not shift when a
   profession's slot captions happen to be longer. */
.nc-eqp-body {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    padding-top: 6dp;
}

.nc-eqp-doll {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 318dp;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    margin-right: 14dp;
    padding-right: 12dp;
    border-right: 1dp {{bg4}};
}

/* Six rows of three cells, every position emitted. Equal flex on the cells is the whole
   alignment story — the spine and rung centre themselves inside their cell, so nothing here
   depends on a measured pixel. */
.nc-eqp-dollrow {
    flex: 1;
    min-height: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
}

.nc-eqp-cell {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
}

/* Junction: the connecting lines Qud draws with box characters. Three stacked children — spine
   segment, rung, spine segment — so the rung lands at the exact vertical middle from equal
   flex alone. No negative margins and no `position: absolute`: percentage margins here resolve
   against WIDTH, and absolute positioning in this RmlUi build resolves against the PANEL
   rather than the nearest positioned ancestor. */
.nc-eqp-spine {
    flex: 1;
    width: 1dp;
    min-height: 0;
    background-color: {{bg4}};
}

.nc-eqp-rung {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 100%;
    height: 1dp;
    background-color: {{bg4}};
}

/* A slot box. Empty slots keep their border and caption, dimmed: "no gloves" is information,
   so an absent item must not become an absent cell. */
.nc-eqp-slot {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    width: 96dp;
    padding: 3dp 2dp;
    border: 1dp {{bg2}};
    background-color: {{bg}};
    transition: border-color 0.15s quadratic-in-out;
}

.nc-eqp-cell.filled .nc-eqp-slot {
    border-color: {{bg4}};
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
}

/* Art box always present, so a slot with a sprite and a slot without are the same height and
   the doll grid does not jitter as the selection changes. */
.nc-eqp-art {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 32dp;
    height: 32dp;
}

.nc-eqp-item {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-size: 11dp;
    text-align: center;
    word-break: break-word;
}

.nc-eqp-slotname {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-size: 9dp;
    letter-spacing: 1dp;
    text-transform: uppercase;
}

.nc-eqp-more {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-size: 10dp;
}

/* Tree. Rows are a FLAT list carrying level classes; uniform height is load-bearing, because
   the cursor's scroll position is computed as scroll_height / row_count in C++. */
.nc-eqp-tree {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    overflow-y: auto;
}

/* `overflow: hidden` is LOAD-BEARING. A long item name wrapped to a second line inside this
   fixed 19dp box and spilled into the row below, so consecutive rows drew on top of each other
   and the whole tree read as garbage. Uniform height is also what makes the cursor's scroll
   position exact arithmetic in C++, so growing the row is not an option. */
.nc-eqp-row {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    height: 19dp;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0 6dp 0 26dp;
    font-size: 13dp;
    overflow: hidden;
    transition: background-color 0.14s quadratic-in-out;
}

/* Indentation states the level; the marker states whether it opens. */
.nc-eqp-row.group {
    padding-left: 2dp;
    font-weight: bold;
    letter-spacing: 1dp;
    margin-top: 3dp;
}

.nc-eqp-row.sub {
    padding-left: 14dp;
}

.nc-eqp-row.focused {
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
    border-left: 3dp {{yellow}};
    padding-left: 23dp;
}

.nc-eqp-row.group.focused {
    padding-left: 0;
}

.nc-eqp-row.sub.focused {
    padding-left: 11dp;
}

.nc-eqp-row.group:hover, .nc-eqp-row.sub:hover {
    background-color: {{bg1}};
}

.nc-eqp-marker {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 12dp;
}

/* `white-space: nowrap` stops the wrap; the ROW's `overflow: hidden` does the clipping. The
   name must NOT carry `overflow: hidden` itself — that makes it a scroll container, whose
   intrinsic width RmlUi resolves to zero, and every label in the tree disappeared while the
   numeric columns kept rendering. */
.nc-eqp-name {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    max-width: 44%;
    margin-right: 6dp;
    white-space: nowrap;
}

/* Leader rule: carries a HEADER's weight across the gap to it, the job Qud gives a run of
   dashes. flex:1 so it absorbs every width the columns do not claim. Transparent on item rows —
   drawn there it struck through every value in the list. */
.nc-eqp-leader {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 8dp;
    height: 1dp;
    margin-right: 8dp;
}

.nc-eqp-row.group .nc-eqp-leader, .nc-eqp-row.sub .nc-eqp-leader {
    background-color: {{bg2}};
}

/* Fixed-width numeric columns, so figures line up down the list rather than ragging with the
   name beside them. */
.nc-eqp-stat {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 68dp;
    font-size: 12dp;
    text-align: right;
}

.nc-eqp-vol {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 62dp;
    font-size: 12dp;
    text-align: right;
}

.nc-eqp-val {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 72dp;
    font-size: 12dp;
    text-align: right;
}

.nc-eqp-wt {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 72dp;
    text-align: right;
}

/* Capacity readout: the number, then a proportional bar, then the close hint. */
.nc-eqp-foot {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 5dp 0 3dp 0;
    border-top: 1dp {{bg4}};
}

/* Used, divider, capacity — three elements so the spacing survives parse. */
.nc-eqp-used {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-size: 14dp;
    font-weight: bold;
}

.nc-eqp-slash {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin: 0 5dp;
    font-size: 14dp;
    color: {{bg4}};
}

.nc-eqp-cap {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-right: 12dp;
    font-size: 14dp;
}

/* FIXED width, not `flex: 1`. The fill's width arrives from C++ and a percentage against a
   flex-sized parent resolved to nothing; against a definite 320dp track a dp length is exact.
   NC_EQP_BAR_DP in build_nc_eqp_sheet is this minus the 1dp borders — keep them in step. */
.nc-eqp-bar {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 320dp;
    height: 9dp;
    background-color: {{bg1}};
    border: 1dp {{bg3}};
}

/* Absorbs the leftover width so the close hint stays hard right now that the bar is fixed. */
.nc-eqp-spacer {
    flex: 1;
    min-width: 0;
}

/* `display: block` is REQUIRED. RmlUi elements default to `display: inline`, and .nc-eqp-bar is
   a plain block rather than a flex container, so this child was never blockified: width and
   height were ignored and the fill drew nothing at all. Every other bare div in this sheet gets
   blockified by a flex parent, which is why this was the only one affected. */
.nc-eqp-bar-fill {
    display: block;
    width: 0;
    height: 100%;
    background-color: {{yellow}};
    transition: width 0.18s quadratic-out;
}

/* Over capacity is a real problem, not a style choice: the bar says so before the number is
   read. */
.nc-eqp-bar.over .nc-eqp-bar-fill {
    background-color: {{red}};
}

.nc-eqp-close {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin-left: 14dp;
    font-size: 12dp;
}

/* Opens the sheet, so it must look like a control rather than status text. Same treatment as
   .nc-tree-all — quiet border, gold on hover — because it is the same kind of thing: a
   screen-level toggle in a status row. */
.nc-prof-eqp {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    margin-left: 16dp;
    padding: 2dp 9dp;
    border: 1dp {{bg4}};
    color: {{fg4}};
    transition: background-color 0.16s quadratic-in-out, border-color 0.16s quadratic-in-out;
}

.nc-prof-eqp:hover {
    background-color: {{bg-hard}};
    border-color: {{yellow}};
}

.nc-prof-eqp-label {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    font-size: 13dp;
    letter-spacing: 1dp;
    text-transform: uppercase;
}
